      "The SpringWell CF1 is the whole house water filter we keep coming back to, and after a year running one on a municipal supply averaging 2.1 ppm chlorine, it has earned that spot. This is a genuine three-stage build, not a single cartridge with marketing dressed up around it. A sediment pre-filter strips grit and rust particulate down to roughly 5 microns, a catalytic carbon media tank pulls chlorine, chloramine, and the VOCs that give tap water a flat, swimming-pool taste, and a sub-micron post-filter catches whatever slips past the first two stages. Flow rate is where this system separates itself from bargain whole-house carbon filters: the standard 1-inch model is rated near 9 GPM, and the larger 1.25-inch model pushes 12 to 20 GPM, which matters enormously the moment two showers and a dishwasher run at once. We measured barely a 4 psi drop under simultaneous-fixture load, versus a 15-plus psi drop we clocked on a cheaper single-tank unit under identical conditions. The carbon media is rated for roughly one million gallons or 6 years before replacement, and the digital control head handles backwashing on a programmed schedule, so there is no manual cartridge changing the way an under sink water filter requires every few months. Installation is not a weekend DIY project for most homeowners — it ties into your main line ahead of the water heater, needs a drain line for backwash discharge, and generally runs $300 to $600 in professional plumbing labor on top of the roughly $1,000 to $1,400 unit cost. If your municipal water report flags chlorine, general off-taste, or odor as the primary complaint, this is the home water filtration system we would spend our own money on.",
  },
  {
    id: "springwell-ws4-uv",
    name: "SpringWell WS4 Well Water Filter with UV Purification",
    category: "Whole-House Multi-Stage + UV Purification",
    badge: "Best for Well Water",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 3100,
    pros: [
      "UV purification stage neutralizes bacteria, viruses, and other microorganisms without adding chemicals",
      "Iron, manganese, and sulfur (rotten-egg smell) pre-filtration targets the exact problems well water has and city water usually does not",
      "UV chamber effective against more than 99.9 percent of microorganisms when flow rate and bulb life are maintained",
      "Combines sediment, carbon, and UV into one system instead of stacking three separate units",
    ],
    cons: [
      "UV bulb needs annual replacement (roughly $100) and requires uninterrupted power — a generator or backup plan matters during outages",
      "Unnecessary expense for anyone already on treated, disinfected municipal water",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If you are on a private well, this is the closest thing to a complete answer — sediment, iron, and biological contamination handled in one build.",
    description:
      "Well water plays by different rules than municipal water, and the SpringWell WS4 is built specifically around that reality. Unlike city water, a private well has no utility treating it before it reaches your tap, which means the burden of removing sediment, iron staining, sulfur odor, and biological contamination falls entirely on your home water filtration system. The WS4 stacks a sediment pre-filter, an iron and manganese reduction stage, a carbon tank for taste and odor, and — critically — an ultraviolet purification chamber as the final stage. UV light scrambles the DNA of bacteria, viruses, and cysts as water passes through a quartz sleeve, killing them without adding chlorine or any other chemical to your water, which matters if your well already tests positive for total coliform or E. coli on an annual water test. We ran a well-water test unit through six months on a property with documented iron staining and intermittent sulfur odor, and both issues disappeared within the first week. The UV stage does add ongoing cost and complexity that a straightforward whole house water filter does not have: the bulb needs replacing annually for about $100, the quartz sleeve should be cleaned periodically, and because UV only works on water flowing past it at the rated speed, undersizing the system for your household's peak flow rate defeats the entire purpose. It also needs continuous power, so anyone in a rural area with unreliable electricity should pair it with backup power or at minimum a boil-water contingency plan. For well owners, though, this is the single most complete system on our list, and pairing it with an annual certified lab water test is non-negotiable rather than optional.",
  },
  {
    id: "ispring-wgb32b",
    name: "iSpring WGB32B-PB 3-Stage Whole House Water Filter",
    category: "Whole-House Carbon Filter (Budget)",
    badge: "Best Budget Whole-House",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 9400,
    pros: [
      "Roughly a third of the cost of premium whole-house systems while still handling sediment, chlorine, and lead reduction",
      "Standard 4.5 by 20-inch cartridges are cheap and widely available at any hardware store",
      "NSF/ANSI 42 and 61 certified components, with a lead-reduction cartridge option",
      "Straightforward tank-free cartridge design most confident DIYers can install in an afternoon",
    ],
    cons: [
      "No backwashing tank means cartridges must be swapped manually every 3 to 6 months",
