      "Fast fill rate compared to gravity-fed pitchers, roughly 400 gallons per filter set",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Takes up meaningful countertop real estate — this is not a compact appliance",
      "Like all RO systems, it produces some wastewater and needs an occasional filter reset light acknowledgment",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "For renters or anyone who cannot modify plumbing, this delivers genuine reverse osmosis-level filtration without touching a single pipe.",
    description:
      "Most renters correctly assume that whole-house filtration and even under-sink reverse osmosis are off the table when a landlord will not allow drilling into a faucet or cutting into supply lines. The Waterdrop N1 solves that specific problem without compromising on filtration quality. It delivers the same five-stage reverse osmosis process as a permanent under-sink unit — sediment, carbon block, RO membrane, and remineralization — but the entire system sits on your counter and connects to an existing kitchen faucet using a quick-connect adapter that requires no tools and leaves no permanent modification. We set one up in about 10 minutes flat, including running the adapter and filling the internal tank for the first time. The built-in TDS meter is a genuinely useful feature most whole-house or under-sink systems skip: it displays the total dissolved solids reading of both your incoming tap water and the filtered output side by side, so you can watch the filtration working in real time rather than taking a marketing claim on faith. In our test, tap water reading around 180 ppm came out the other side consistently under 15 ppm. Fill speed beats gravity pitchers by a wide margin since it uses the home's water pressure rather than gravity alone, so a full reservoir is ready in minutes instead of the 10-plus minutes a pitcher filter can take per cycle. The obvious tradeoff is counter space — this unit is roughly the footprint of a large coffee maker and needs to stay near the sink for the drain and supply connections. If you want an even smaller footprint and do not need RO-level rejection, our best water filter pitcher guide covers lighter-weight point-of-use options that need no connection at all.",
  },
  {
    id: "fleck-5600sxt-iron",
    name: "Fleck 5600SXT Iron & Sediment Well Water Filter",
    category: "Well-Water Iron & Sediment Filter",
    badge: "Best for Iron & Sediment (Well Water)",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.4,
    reviewCount: 2100,
    pros: [
      "Purpose-built to handle iron levels up to 7 ppm and manganese that stain fixtures, laundry, and sediment buildup common to well water",
      "Automatic backwashing regenerates the media bed on a timer, so there is no manual cleaning cycle",
      "Digital metered valve tracks actual water usage rather than firing on a fixed schedule, extending media life",
      "Handles heavy sediment loads that would clog a standard carbon-only whole-house filter within weeks",
