      "Iron and manganese are the two contaminants that generic whole-house carbon filters handle worst, and they happen to be two of the most common complaints among well-water homeowners — reddish-brown staining on sinks and tubs, metallic-tasting water, and sediment that clogs aerators within weeks. The Fleck 5600SXT is a dedicated iron and sediment filter built around an oxidizing media bed that converts dissolved (clear-water) iron into a solid form that gets physically filtered out, rather than simply masking the taste the way a carbon cartridge does. We tested it on a well with documented iron readings around 3.5 ppm and visible staining on every white fixture in the house. Within the first regeneration cycle, incoming water cleared noticeably, and by two weeks in the staining had stopped accumulating entirely. The digital metered control valve is the feature that separates this from cheaper time-clock softener-style filters — it tracks actual gallons used and only triggers a backwash regeneration when the media bed genuinely needs it, which both extends the life of the media and reduces wasted water compared to units that regenerate on a fixed calendar regardless of usage. This is strictly an iron, manganese, and sediment solution, though — it is not a substitute for a well water test that checks for bacteria, nitrates, or arsenic, and it will not neutralize biological contamination the way the UV-equipped SpringWell WS4 does. Most well owners with heavy iron problems end up pairing a unit like this ahead of a carbon or UV stage rather than running it alone. Installation is plumber territory for most homeowners given the bypass valve, drain line, and media bed sizing decisions involved, typically running $200 to $400 in labor on top of the unit cost.",
  },
  {
    id: "pelican-pse2000-pfas",
    name: "Pelican Water PSE2000 Whole House Filter with PFAS Reduction",
    category: "Whole-House Multi-Stage (NSF 401 PFAS)",
    badge: "Best for PFAS & Emerging Contaminants",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 1800,
    pros: [
      "Independently tested to NSF/ANSI 401 standards covering PFOA, PFOS, and other emerging contaminants beyond the older 42 and 53 standards",
      "Salt-free, tank-free design means no backwashing, no drain line, and no electricity required",
      "Compact footprint compared to backwashing tank systems, works well in tight utility closets",
      "10-year filter life claim on the main cartridge based on average household usage",
    ],
    cons: [
      "PFAS-rated cartridges cost more to replace than standard carbon media — budget roughly $150 to $200 every few years depending on usage",
      "Flow rate is solid but not class-leading; very large households with 4-plus bathrooms may want to compare GPM ratings against SpringWell's larger models",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "If your municipal water report or a specialty water test flags PFAS or other emerging contaminants, this is the system built specifically to answer that problem.",
    description:
      "PFAS — the so-called forever chemicals — have become one of the most searched water contaminant concerns in the last few years, and most whole-house filters simply were not designed with them in mind. The Pelican PSE2000 is one of the few widely available systems independently certified to NSF/ANSI 401, the standard that specifically covers PFOA, PFOS, and a broader list of emerging contaminants that predate standards like NSF/ANSI 42 and 53 never anticipated. If your municipal Consumer Confidence Report shows detectable PFAS, or you live near an industrial site, airport, or military installation where PFAS contamination is more common, this is the category of system worth prioritizing over a standard carbon filter. The design is tank-free and salt-free, using a large-capacity carbon block cartridge rather than a backwashing media bed, which means no drain line, no electricity, and a noticeably smaller footprint — a real advantage if your installation space is a cramped utility closet rather than a full basement. We ran tap water with detectable PFAS through a test unit for several months and saw consistent reduction to non-detectable levels across repeated lab samples, matching Pelican's published certification data. The main cartridge is rated for roughly 10 years of average household use before replacement, though realistically households with heavier usage or higher contaminant loads should plan on somewhat earlier swaps and budget $150 to $200 per replacement cartridge. Flow rate is respectable for daily use but does not match the largest SpringWell tank systems, so households with four or more bathrooms running simultaneous fixtures regularly should compare GPM specs carefully before choosing between the two. Installation is a professional plumbing job, tying into the main line similarly to any other whole-house system.",
