          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Whole-House Filtration Matters
          </h2>

          <p>
            A pitcher filter or a single faucet attachment treats the water you drink. A <strong>whole house water filter</strong> treats every drop that enters your home — the water in your shower, your washing machine, your ice maker, and the water your kids splash around in the bath. That distinction matters more than most people realize. Chlorine and chloramine used to disinfect municipal water don't just affect taste; they volatilize into steam during hot showers, and sediment and scale accumulate in water heaters, dishwashers, and pipe fittings over years, quietly shortening the life of every appliance that touches water in your home.
          </p>

          <p>
            A quality <strong>home water filtration system</strong> installed at the point where water enters your house addresses all of that at once. Sediment stops clogging aerators and showerheads. Chlorine taste and smell disappear from tap water, ice, and cooking. Depending on the system, it can also reduce lead, PFAS, and — for well-water homes — iron, manganese, and biological contamination that municipal treatment plants would otherwise have handled for you. The tradeoff is cost and installation complexity: a proper whole-house system is a plumbing project, not a countertop appliance, and it is genuinely overkill for someone whose only complaint is that their drinking water tastes a little flat.
          </p>

