        <!-- SECTION 2: Types of Filtration -->
        <div id="types-of-filtration" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Types of Whole-House Filtration Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Not every filtration technology belongs at the point of entry to your home. Some are built for whole-house flow rates; others are fundamentally point-of-use technologies that don't scale up the way marketing sometimes implies. Here's the honest breakdown.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Sediment Pre-Filtration</h3>
          <p>
            The first stage in nearly every serious system. A sediment pre-filter physically screens out sand, rust particulate, and silt before it reaches downstream stages or your appliances. It's cheap, it's simple, and skipping it shortens the life of every other filter stage and every fixture in the house.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Activated Carbon</h3>
          <p>
            The workhorse of home water filtration. Carbon media (granular or catalytic carbon block) adsorbs chlorine, chloramine, and the volatile organic compounds responsible for most taste and odor complaints. This is what makes a <strong>whole house water filter</strong> feel like a genuine upgrade the day it's installed — the chlorine smell in the shower just disappears. Carbon alone does not meaningfully remove dissolved solids like fluoride, arsenic, or most heavy metals.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Reverse Osmosis</h3>
          <p>
            Reverse osmosis pushes water through a semi-permeable membrane fine enough to reject up to 99 percent of dissolved contaminants, including lead, arsenic, fluoride, nitrates, and most PFAS. It's the most comprehensive filtration technology available for residential use — but here's the part most guides gloss over: <strong>true whole-house reverse osmosis is rare and expensive</strong>, because RO membranes are inherently slow. Building a system that can push whole-house flow rates of 10-plus GPM through RO membranes requires a large, costly setup that most homes simply don't need. An <strong>under sink water filter</strong> using RO, treating just your drinking and cooking water, delivers the same contaminant removal at a fraction of the cost and footprint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">UV Purification</h3>
          <p>
            Ultraviolet light neutralizes bacteria, viruses, and cysts by disrupting their DNA as water passes through a UV chamber. It adds no chemicals and no taste, but it only treats microbiological contamination — it does nothing for sediment, chlorine, or dissolved minerals. UV stages are mainly relevant for well water, since municipal water is already disinfected before it reaches your home.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Combination Multi-Stage Systems</h3>
          <p>
            Most of the systems worth buying combine two or more of the above — sediment plus carbon at minimum, sometimes with UV or a dedicated iron/manganese stage layered in for well water. The right combination depends entirely on what a water test actually shows is in your water, which is why we cover testing before the product reviews.
          </p>

