        <!-- SECTION 3: NSF Certifications -->
        <div id="nsf-certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What NSF Certifications Actually Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            NSF/ANSI certification numbers show up on nearly every product page in this category, and most shoppers skim right past them without understanding what each one actually promises. That's a mistake, because the certification number tells you precisely what a system was independently tested to remove — and, just as importantly, what it wasn't.
          </p>

          <ul>
            <li><strong>NSF/ANSI 42 (Aesthetic Effects):</strong> Covers chlorine taste, odor, and particulate reduction. This is the baseline certification for most carbon filters — it tells you the water will taste and smell better, but it says nothing about health-related contaminants.</li>
            <li><strong>NSF/ANSI 53 (Health Effects):</strong> A more rigorous standard covering contaminants with documented health effects, including lead, cysts like giardia and cryptosporidium, and certain VOCs. A filter certified to 53 is making a health claim, not just a taste claim.</li>
            <li><strong>NSF/ANSI 58 (Reverse Osmosis Systems):</strong> Specific to RO systems, covering reduction of total dissolved solids and a range of contaminants that only RO membranes can effectively remove.</li>
            <li><strong>NSF/ANSI 401 (Emerging Contaminants):</strong> The newest and most relevant standard for concerns like PFAS/PFOA, pharmaceuticals, and pesticides that older standards never anticipated. If PFAS is a concern in your area, this is the certification number to look for specifically.</li>
          </ul>

          <p>
            A system can legitimately advertise removal of a contaminant based on lab testing without holding formal NSF certification, but independent certification means an accredited third party verified the manufacturer's claims rather than taking them at face value. When comparing any <strong>reverse osmosis system</strong> or whole-house filter, matching the certification number to the specific contaminant you're worried about is far more useful than any marketing headline on the box.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 4: What's Actually in Your Water -->
        <div id="whats-in-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. What's Actually in Your Water? Test Before You Buy
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the step nearly every homeowner skips, and it's the single biggest reason people end up disappointed with a new filtration system. Buying a chlorine-focused carbon filter when your actual problem is high iron, or spending on a whole-house PFAS-rated system when your water report shows nothing of the sort, wastes money in both directions. The right system depends entirely on what's actually coming out of your tap.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You're on Municipal Water</h3>
          <p>
            Every public water utility in the United States is legally required to publish an annual Consumer Confidence Report (sometimes called a water quality report) detailing what was tested and what levels were found, including chlorine, lead, disinfection byproducts, and any contaminants that approached or exceeded EPA limits. Most utilities post this online, searchable by your utility name and year, and it costs nothing to look up. Start there before buying anything.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">If You're on a Private Well</h3>
          <p>
            Well water has no utility testing it for you, which makes an independent lab water test essential rather than optional — and it should be repeated annually, since well water quality can shift with seasons, rainfall, and nearby agricultural activity. At minimum, test for total coliform bacteria, nitrates, iron, manganese, pH, and hardness. If your property is near agricultural land, a gas station, or an industrial site, add nitrates, arsenic, and PFAS to the panel. Home test kits exist and are fine for a rough screen, but a certified lab test is the only way to make a confident purchasing decision for a system that will cost hundreds or thousands of dollars.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Test First, Buy Second</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              A $15 to $50 water test tells you exactly what contaminants are present and at what concentration. Skipping it means guessing at a purchase that can cost anywhere from $150 for a countertop unit to $2,000 for a premium whole-house system with professional installation. Test first — every single time.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

