        <div id="installation-maintenance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Installation, Flow Rate & Maintenance
          </h2>

          <p>
            A whole-house system is only as good as its installation and upkeep. Get either wrong and you'll end up with weak water pressure, a filter that stopped working months ago, or both.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Professional Install vs. DIY</h3>
          <p>
            Whole-house systems tie directly into your main water line, typically installed in a basement, crawlspace, garage, or dedicated utility closet before the water heater — so every fixture in the house benefits, including hot water lines. This involves cutting into copper or PEX supply lines, installing a bypass valve so the household has water during future filter service, and in the case of backwashing systems, running a dedicated drain line for the regeneration cycle. Confident DIYers with plumbing experience can handle cartridge-style systems like the iSpring WGB32B-PB; tank-style systems with backwashing valves and drain requirements — like the SpringWell CF1, the WS4, or the Fleck 5600SXT — are generally worth the $150 to $600 in professional labor to avoid leaks, incorrect bypass wiring, or code issues. Under-sink and countertop units, by contrast, are realistic DIY projects for nearly anyone in under two hours.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Flow Rate and Water Pressure</h3>
          <p>
            This is the single most overlooked spec in this entire category. A filtration system's flow rate, measured in gallons per minute (GPM), determines how much pressure drop you'll notice when multiple fixtures run at once. As a rule of thumb, a typical single-bathroom home needs a system rated for at least 7 to 9 GPM, a standard 2 to 3 bathroom home should look for 9 to 12 GPM, and larger homes with 4 or more bathrooms or simultaneous high-demand fixtures (irrigation, multiple showers) should prioritize systems rated 12 to 20 GPM. Undersizing a system is the number one cause of the complaint we see most often: "our water pressure dropped after installing a filter." Always check a system's rated GPM against your home's peak simultaneous demand before buying, not just its average usage.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Filter and Cartridge Replacement Schedules</h3>
          <p>
            Replacement intervals vary enormously by system type. Standard sediment pre-filters typically need swapping every 3 to 6 months. Carbon cartridge stages in budget systems run 6 to 12 months depending on water quality and usage. Tank-style carbon media in premium systems can last 5 to 10 years before replacement, since it's a much larger volume of media measured in cubic feet rather than a small cartridge. Reverse osmosis membranes typically last 2 to 3 years, while the sediment and carbon prefilters ahead of an RO membrane need replacement every 6 to 12 months. UV bulbs need annual replacement regardless of how much water passes through, since their germicidal output degrades over time even if the bulb still lights up. Missing a replacement schedule doesn't just mean reduced filtration — an overdue cartridge can become a breeding ground for the exact bacteria you installed the system to avoid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7: Which System Do You Need -->
        <div id="which-system" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Whole-House vs. Under-Sink vs. Pitcher: Which Do You Actually Need?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This guide is focused specifically on whole-house filtration — the biggest, most comprehensive tier of water treatment, installed at the point where water enters your home. But whole-house filtration isn't the right answer for every water problem, and it's worth being honest about when a smaller, cheaper system actually solves what you're dealing with.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your issue is <strong>hard water</strong> — scale buildup on faucets and showerheads, soap that won't lather, film on dishes, or spots on glassware — that's a mineral hardness problem caused by calcium and magnesium, and no carbon or sediment filter will fix it. You need a water softener, which uses an entirely different ion-exchange process. Our best water softener system guide covers that category in depth, including how softening and filtration can be combined in one plumbing run when a home has both hardness and contamination concerns.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your only complaint is that your <strong>drinking water tastes slightly off</strong> and you have no interest in a plumbing project, a point-of-use option is the more sensible starting point. Our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">best water filter pitcher guide</a> covers lightweight, zero-installation options that polish drinking water taste without touching a single pipe — ideal for renters, small households, or anyone who just wants better-tasting water from the fridge.
          </p>

          <p>
            Whole-house filtration earns its cost and complexity when the problem is systemic — sediment clogging every fixture, chlorine smell in every shower, or contaminants like lead, PFAS, or well-water bacteria that you don't want touching your skin, your laundry, or your ice cubes, not just your drinking glass. If that describes your situation, the systems reviewed above are where to start.
          </p>
        </div>

