              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best whole house water filtration system for home use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homeowners on municipal water, the SpringWell CF1 offers the best balance of filtration performance, flow rate, and low maintenance. It uses a three-stage sediment and carbon design that handles chlorine, chloramine, and sediment while maintaining strong water pressure. Well-water households should look at a system with UV purification, like the SpringWell WS4, since municipal disinfection is not present in well water.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a whole-house filter if I already have a water softener?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, they solve different problems. A water softener removes calcium and magnesium minerals that cause hardness, scale buildup, and soap scum, but it does not remove chlorine, sediment, lead, or other contaminants. A whole-house water filtration system handles filtration while a softener handles hardness. Many homes with hard water and contamination concerns run both in sequence. See our best water softener system guide for hardness-specific recommendations.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to install a whole-house water filtration system?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget carbon systems start around $300 to $500 for the unit alone, while premium tank-style systems with backwashing run $1,000 to $1,800. Professional installation typically adds $150 to $600 depending on your plumbing layout, drain access, and whether a bypass valve and pressure regulator need to be added. Under-sink and countertop systems are far cheaper, generally $150 to $400 total including any installation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a whole-house water filter reduce my water pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can, if the system's flow rate (measured in GPM) is undersized for your household's peak demand. A system rated for 6 GPM will noticeably choke water pressure in a home where two showers and a washing machine commonly run at once. Match the system's rated GPM to your home's fixture count and peak simultaneous usage, and favor systems rated 10 GPM or higher for larger households.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is reverse osmosis necessary for a whole-house system, or should I just get an under-sink unit?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                True whole-house reverse osmosis is rare and expensive because RO membranes are inherently slow, making high-flow-rate whole-house RO systems large and costly. For nearly all homes, an under-sink reverse osmosis system treating drinking and cooking water is the practical choice, paired with a separate whole-house sediment and carbon filter for the rest of the house.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I know what contaminants are actually in my water before buying a filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Municipal water customers can request their utility's annual Consumer Confidence Report, which lists tested contaminant levels. Well water owners should get an independent lab water test at least once a year, checking for bacteria, nitrates, iron, manganese, arsenic, and pH at minimum. Buying a filtration system before testing your water is the most common mistake homeowners make in this category.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes That Waste Money on Water Filtration
          </h2>

          <p>
            After reviewing hundreds of customer complaints and installation stories, these are the four mistakes we see homeowners make most often when shopping for a water filtration system for home use.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Whole-House When You Only Needed a Pitcher or Faucet Filter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If your only complaint is that drinking water tastes slightly off, spending $1,500 plus installation on a whole-house system is a wildly disproportionate response. A water filter pitcher or a simple faucet-mounted carbon filter solves taste and light chlorine odor for a fraction of the cost and zero plumbing work. Save whole-house spending for problems that actually affect the entire house — sediment, systemic chlorine odor, or contaminants that concern you across every fixture, not just your drinking glass.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring Flow Rate and Tanking Your Water Pressure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                This is the single most common regret we hear about after installation. A system rated for 6 to 7 GPM feels fine for a single person living alone, but the moment a second bathroom, a washing machine, and a dishwasher all draw water at once in a larger household, pressure collapses noticeably. Before buying, count your bathrooms, factor in laundry and irrigation, and check the system's rated GPM against realistic peak simultaneous demand — not just the average flow a manufacturer advertises under ideal single-fixture conditions.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Testing Your Water Before Choosing a System</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Buying a chlorine-focused carbon system when the real problem is high iron, or spending extra for PFAS-rated filtration when your municipal report shows no detectable PFAS at all, wastes money in both directions. A $15 to $50 water test — either your utility's published Consumer Confidence Report or an independent lab test for well water — tells you precisely what to filter for before you spend hundreds or thousands of dollars guessing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Neglecting the Filter Replacement Schedule</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An overdue sediment or carbon cartridge doesn't just stop filtering effectively — it can become a breeding ground for bacteria, actively making water quality worse than having no filter at all. Set a recurring reminder based on your specific system's replacement interval, whether that's every 3 months for a budget cartridge or every several years for premium tank media, and treat it as a fixed maintenance cost rather than an easy corner to cut.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
